p('Lunar orbit and attitude disturbances','TitleX')
p('Matched comparison with Earth observation orbits','SubX')
p('<b>Quantitative concept study - 29 October 2026</b>')
p('Around the Moon, the large terms from atmospheric drag and magnetic control in low Earth orbit (LEO) disappear. Close lunar gravity, however, still changes the periapsis and produces a substantial gravity-gradient torque. A distant halo orbit reduces the lunar gravity-gradient torque, but the momentum build-up from solar pressure, the unstable dynamics and the need for navigation remain.')
table(['Result','Finding','Design implication'],[
 ['400 / 600 km LEO','Altitude loss in 30 days of 2.2-45 km / 0.06-3.0 km','The atmospheric state and the altitude strongly change the drag demand.'],
 ['100 km lunar polar','Minimum osculating periapsis of 77.3 km in 30 days','The conservative evolution of the eccentricity must be checked.'],
 ['50 km lunar polar','The 11 km radial screening buffer is crossed near day 48','This is a conditional safety trigger, not a terrain impact.'],
 ['Maintained geometry','LRO needed ~143 m/s/year at 50±15 km, against <5 in a frozen geometry [DE05]','Choose the observing envelope and a favourable orbit before sizing the propulsion.'],
 ['Matched gravity gradient','Moon100 / LEO400 = 0.617','The ratio of surface gravities is the wrong scaling for the torque.'],
 ['30 nN m bias','0.025 N m s fills in 9.65 days; ~8 g/year of propellant for unloading','The alignment of the pressure centre and external unloading matter.']
 ],[105,190,205])
fig('orbit_30day_comparison.png',500,200)
p('<b>Status:</b> our own local propagation and analytical calculations are kept separate from published flight and design evidence. No orbit, hardware selection, lifetime, control allocation or D requirement is baselined. The full audit is in the accompanying Markdown and workbook. This supporting report does not enlarge the 50-page budget of the final mission report.','SmallX')
page();head('1. Matched cases and Earth-orbit decay')
p('The reference spacecraft is a uniform 30 kg box of 0.20 × 0.30 × 0.40 m with I = diag(0.625, 0.500, 0.325) kg m². The effective projected area is 0.30 m², with Cd = 2.2 and Cr = 1.5. The pressure-centre offset is 2 cm, and the residual magnetic dipole is 0.030 A m². These are illustrative matched properties, not a bus selection. The box is too small for the illustrative 200 mm optics of D01, so the absolute values of torque, momentum and propellant must be recomputed for a selected spacecraft.')
table(['Case','Initial geometry / frame','Model'],[['LEO400','400 km circular, 51.6°, Ω = u = 0','Mean circular a; drag power with corotation; J2 node'],['LEO600','600 km circular, 98°, Ω = u = 0','Same; the rounded inclination is not an exact sun-synchronous orbit (SSO)'],['Moon100 / Moon50','100 / 50 km circular polar; Ω = u = 0','Lunar principal-axis (PA) equator at the epoch, frozen inertially; Moon-centred J2000 propagation'],['Moon30×200','hp / ha = 30 / 200 km, i=90°, ω=270°','Geometry comparator; not an optimized frozen orbit'],['Distant','JPL northern L2 halo nearest to C=3.09','Autonomous circular restricted three-body problem (CRTBP); no dated ephemeris realization']],[95,205,200])
p('The study epoch for Earth and low lunar orbits is <b>2026-10-29 00:00:00 UTC</b>. Lunar heights refer to the 1738 km gravity reference sphere, and Earth heights to 6378.137 km. The lunar radius and terrain, and the PA and mean-Earth (ME) frame conventions, must always be stated explicitly.')
sub('Executed NRLMSIS 2.0 density sensitivity [DE07]')
p('The first-orbit profiles use 96 locations and times, daily / 81-day F10.7 values of 70 / 140 / 220 sfu and a daily ap of 4 / 15 / 100. The resulting mean density profile is held fixed during the decay. These quiet, moderate and active states are sensitivity fixtures, not a weather forecast for a year. Winds, seasonal and local-time evolution and the full Cartesian short-period motion are omitted.')
table(['Case','ρ [kg/m³]','30-day loss [km]','Held-altitude makeup [m/s/year]'],[[x['name'],f"{x['rho_kg_m3']:.2e}",f"{x['h0_km']-x['h30_km']:.3f}",f"{x['drag_makeup_m_s_year_at_held_alt']:.2f}"]for x in R['leo']],[125,110,110,155])
p('The quiet 400 km case ends at 360.5 km after one year. The moderate and active cases reach the 120 km validity boundary of the model at 148 and 57 days; this is not a simulated impact. The 600 km cases end at 599.2 / 592.5 / 554.6 km after one year. The initial J2 node rates of -5.002°/day and +1.012°/day describe precession, not decay.','SmallX')
page();head('2. Low-lunar evolution and maintenance')
p('Our local propagation uses the GRGM1200A gravity harmonics to 80×80, geometric differential forces of the Sun and Earth from DE440s, direct solar radiation pressure (SRP) and a cylindrical lunar shadow. The DOP853 integrator runs with rtol 2×10⁻⁹, atol 10⁻⁵ on SI components and a maximum step of 120 s. States are saved every 600 s. No atmospheric-style decay law is assumed for the Moon. [DE01-DE04]')
p('The DE430 PA gravity solution uses the available DE421 PA rotation kernel as an explicit approximation, and an orientation offset of 0.01° is tested. Earth shadow, tides, clones from the gravity covariance and a local terrain model are omitted. This is a concept comparison, not an operational certification of the orbit.','SmallX')
table(['Initial case','Day-30 hp / ha [km]','Minimum hp [km]','Interval'],[[x['name'].replace('_N80',''),f"{x['day30'][2]:.2f} / {x['day30'][3]:.2f}",f"{x['min_osculating_hp_km']:.2f}",f"{x['duration_d']:.1f} days"]for x in R['lunar'][:4]],[155,130,100,115])
fig('orbit_extended.png',500,200)
p('In Moon100 the sampled semimajor axis spans 1836.56-1838.34 km, while the periapsis reaches 77.3 km, so the change in orbit shape dominates. The 50 km integration stops at an actual radial crossing of the assumed 11 km screening buffer. This boundary is neither a local terrain impact nor a validated safe envelope, and the crossing time at day 48 is not converged at degree 120.')
sub('Maintained versus uncontrolled evidence')
p('The 50±15 km science orbit of LRO used paired burns to rotate the apsides approximately every 28 days, at about 11 m/s per cycle. Its frozen geometry reduced the yearly maintenance from about 143 m/s to less than 5 m/s. Older design cases at 50 / 100 km reached a 12 km mountain envelope in about 41 / 150 days. Their initial geometry, radius convention and force model differ from this study. [DE05-DE06]')
p('A run that survives 30 days establishes neither a one-year lifetime nor zero maintenance. The observed inclination trend of LRO (~0.4°/year) also shows why polar exposure must allow for or control the drift of the orbit plane. Visibility of the full surface and usable night exposure remain to be demonstrated.','SmallX')
page();head('3. Distant halo: stability and corrections')
p(f'The reproduced long-period northern L2 halo from JPL has C = {H["jacobi"]:.12f}, a period of {H["period_d"]:.4f} days and a Moon-centre range of {H["range_km"][0]:,.0f}-{H["range_km"][1]:,.0f} km. It is a nearby comparator from the LUMIO family, not the dated operational trajectory of LUMIO. [DE08]')
p('The initial normalized state is [1.1589028132, 0, 0.1274150464, 0, -0.2099714489, 0]. The exact state, the mass ratio, the length and time units and the catalogue response are retained. The rotating barycentric x axis points toward the Moon, and z follows the angular momentum of the Earth-Moon system.')
fig('distant_halo_drift.png',500,210)
table(['Test','Result','Meaning'],[['Initial offsets','+1 km x, +0.01 m/s vy','All other state offsets zero'],['Uncontrolled departure at day 30',f'{H["uncontrolled_day30_deviation_km"]:,.0f} km','Deviation from the reference, not a lunar decay rate'],['Unstable multiplier / e-folding',f'{H["unstable_multiplier_fd"]:.1f} / {H["linear_efold_d"]:.2f} days','Local linear sensitivity; depends on phase'],['Ideal weekly targeting',f'≤1 km; {H["ideal_targeting_dv30_m_s"]:.4f} m/s in 30 days','Exact navigation and execution; initial error only']],[140,150,210])
p('The ideal controller targets the position 14 days downstream and applies velocity impulses every 7 days. It omits the Sun, SRP, the eccentricity of the Earth-Moon orbit, continuous model error and stochastic tracking errors. Its small cost must therefore not be annualized into a stationkeeping budget.')
p('The 2024 LUMIO design gives an annual mean of 8.256 m/s and 15.79 m/s at 3σ with a planning cut-off of 2 days. An earlier design from 2018 gave 28.1 m/s at 3σ under different assumptions. We retain <b>16-30 m/s/year as a provisional sensitivity</b>, a bracket from two dated studies, until a dated navigation and control design exists. [DE09, DE11]')
p('The 2026 transfer study keeps a generating halo with Cj=3.09, and ESA reports Phase C and a possible launch in 2028. This is current design and schedule evidence, not flight performance. A single farside observer at L2 does not on its own establish the coverage of the entire surface that the project requires. [DE12-DE13]','SmallX')
page();head('4. Force comparison and secondary effects')
fig('force_screening.png',500,230)
table(['Mechanism [m/s²]','400 km LEO','100 km lunar','Distant50,000km'],[['Non-spherical gravity','J2 polar bound2.50e-2','Harmonic RMS7.19e-4; sampled max1.96e-3','Lunar J2 ~1.4e-9'],['Earth / Moon tide','Moon bound1.17e-6','Earth RMS1.79e-5','Earth exact anti-Earth5.85e-4'],['Solar tide','Bound5.37e-7','RMS1.13e-7','Bound3.96e-6'],['Sunlit direct SRP','6.81e-8','6.81e-8','6.81e-8']],[150,110,125,115])
p('The force model uses the differential third-body acceleration µ[(R-r)/|R-r|³ - R/|R|³]. The distant dynamics require the full multi-body equations. Integrating the magnitude of a conservative acceleration does not give a maintenance Δv.')
sub('Numerical screening of smaller effects')
p('A screen of the ambient lunar exosphere carries the upper surface estimate of 2×10⁵ particles/cm³ up to orbital altitude and treats every particle as 40 u. It gives ρ=1.33×10⁻¹⁴ kg/m³, an acceleration of 3.90×10⁻¹⁰ m/s² and a loss of about 2.3 m in circular semimajor axis in 30 days at 100 km. This is neither an altitude profile nor a bound on the plumes of nearby landers. [DE14]')
p('A 2 W anisotropic thermal beam produces a force of 6.67 nN. An assumed leak of 10⁻¹⁰ kg/s at 1000 m/s produces a force of 100 nN and a torque of 10 nN m at 10 cm, which can matter for the secular momentum. These are sensitivities to effects generated by the spacecraft, not measured mission properties.')
p('Solar-wind screening (5 cm⁻³, 400 km/s) gives a pressure of 1.34 nPa and a torque of 8 pN m. A conditional impact of 10⁻⁹ kg at 20 km/s on a 20 cm arm gives an angular impulse of 4×10⁻⁶ N m s. We claim no annual impact rate, damage probability or simulation with the Meteoroid Engineering Model (MEM). [DE24]','SmallX')
page();head('5. Environmental torque budget')
p('For a central body the gravity-gradient (GG) torque is τ = 3µ/r³ [r̂ × I r̂]. Its envelope over arbitrary attitudes is 1.5µ/r³(Imax-Imin), with the maximum near 45° between the extreme principal axes. Ideal nadir alignment of a principal axis removes this term, but a constant misalignment can produce a secular torque.')
table(['Case','GG peak [N m]','GG at1° [N m]','SRP sunlit [N m]','Magnetic scenario [N m]'],[[x['case'],f"{x['GG_peak_Nm']:.2e}",f"{x['GG_1deg_Nm']:.2e}",f"{x['SRP_sunlit_bound_Nm']:.2e}",f"{x['magnetic_30mAm2_bound_Nm']:.2e}"]for x in R['torques']],[100,100,100,100,100])
p('<b>Aerodynamic torque:</b> the quiet, moderate and active 400 km cases give 0.283/1.44/4.37 µNm, and 600 km gives 0.0078/0.0739/0.366 µNm. These values use the mean density times the mean relative speed squared and a perpendicular arm of 2 cm; they are not a full attitude or RMS history. The screen of the ambient exosphere at 100 km above the Moon gives 0.234 nNm. Drag can dominate in the active 400 km case.','SmallX')
fig('torque_comparison.png',500,200)
p('The magnetic scenarios are 50 µT in LEO and 30 nT at the Moon, with lunar sensitivities of 5 and 100 nT. They are declared engineering cases, not a propagated World Magnetic Model (WMM) or crustal field, and not a certified percentile. A 0.2 A m² torquer provides 10 µN m in 50 µT but only 6 nN m at 30 nT. That is below the 30 nN m bias example, and there is no authority parallel to B. [DE15-DE16, DE22]')
p('At a lunar range of 100 km, the screening of reflected light gives 4.38 nN m, and the deliberately conservative hot infrared envelope gives 21.4 nN m. A nightside surface at 95 K instead gives ~0.083 nN m with the same assumptions for view and arm. Incoming planetary radiation is distinct from the thermal recoil of the spacecraft. The actual phase, plate normals and surface temperatures set the mean and the RMS. [DE14, DE23]')
p('These bounds are not simultaneous vector peaks. The distant 50k row also needs the gravity-gradient tensors of the Earth and Sun, which give about 2.2×10⁻¹² / 1.8×10⁻¹⁴ N m at the stated collinear geometry. No history of wheel storage in the halo is inferred from a circular lunar surrogate.','SmallX')
page();head('6. Momentum, unloading and propulsion')
p('For a fixed inertial axis, a periodic torque T sin2nt gives a momentum amplitude of T/(2n), not T multiplied by the mission duration. The Moon100 example has an amplitude of 0.200 mN m s and the LEO400 example 0.255 mN m s. In rotating body axes, ω×H and the commanded body momentum must be included.')
table(['Bias [N m]','Cadence [days]','RCS impulse [N s/year]','Propellant [g/year]','Dump-only lost time'],[[f"{x['bias_Nm']:.0e}",f"{x['cadence_d']:.2f}",f"{x['annual_impulse_Ns']:.2f}",f"{x['annual_prop_g']:.2f}",f"{100*x['science_loss_fraction_20min_dump']:.3f}%"]for x in S['unloading']],[90,85,120,100,105])
p('We assume 0.025 N m s of usable storage per axis, two opposite 1 mN thrusters of the reaction control system (RCS), each 15 cm from the centre of mass (COM), an Isp of 80 s and 20 min per dump including recovery. Each dump requires 83 s of thrusting and a summed impulse of 0.167 N s. A balanced couple gives zero net Δv; a single unbalanced thruster gives 5.56 mm/s, and a 1% mismatch of the pair ~0.028 mm/s.')
fig('momentum_pointing.png',500,200)
p('A triangular slew of 10° in 60 s requires 0.121 mN m and 3.64 mN m s on the axis with 0.625 kgm². Reserving this slew inside the allocation of 0.025 Nms shortens the cadence for the 30 nNm bias to 8.24 days. A constant nadir tracking error of 1° can add a lunar GG bias of 12.4 nNm, and its effect must be treated in the correct attitude and frame.')
p('Component checks: the CW0500 wheel gives 50 mNms at 6000 rpm, 10 mNm and 322 g per wheel, so four wheels alone weigh 1.29 kg. The NANO R3 thruster gives 350 µN at 45 W nominal; 16-30 m/s for 30 kg therefore requires about 16-30 days of thrusting per year. It cannot perform an impulsive LRO-style burn of 5.5 m/s in a short apse window. [DE17, DE21]')
p('Maintenance, recovery, unloading, contingency and disposal resources should be kept separate. At an assumed chemical Isp of 220 s, 5/16/30/143 m/s require ~0.069/0.222/0.414/1.92 kg of propellant for an initial mass of 30 kg, before hardware and residuals. The disposal comparators of LUMIO (~1-3 m/s) are specific to their trajectory.','SmallX')
page();head('7. Pointing, image motion and internal vibration')
p('Torque sets the angular acceleration, not the optical jitter or the knowledge accuracy. A 30 nNm bias on 0.5 kgm² gives an open-loop drift of 61.9 arcsec in 100 s. A scalar rigid-body PD controller with a natural frequency of 0.02 Hz and a damping of 0.7 leaves a static bias error of 0.784 arcsec without integral action. Nominal tracking is cancelled by feedforward, and sensor and structural noise are zero in this illustration.')
table(['Measurement link','Conditional result','Required follow-through'],[['Pixel scale','2.005 arcsec/pixel for 4.86 µm / 0.5 m optics','Earlier fixture; not a sensor or optics selection'],['Knowledge projection','1 arcsec→242 m at 50,000 km nadir','Add the navigation, boresight, terrain and off-nadir covariance'],['Residual rate','0.1 deg/s→0.90 pixel in 5 ms; 0.18 in 1 ms','Scene gradient, PSF, thresholds and throughput are needed for the event rate'],['Low-lunar tracking','Nadir body rate 0.0509 deg/s; a fixed-ground line of sight can approach 0.94 deg/s','The tracking geometry differs from the residual jitter'],['Earlier 0.2 arcsec allocation','0.10 pixel displacement in the optical fixture','Not achieved hardware performance']],[120,190,190])
sub('Separate component disturbance evidence [DE18]')
p('Shields et al. measured a historical BCT15 wheel with a static imbalance of 0.38 gmm and a dynamic imbalance of 27.59 gmm². At 3000 rpm these give a fundamental force of ~0.0375 N and a torque of ~0.00272 Nm. The direct rigid-body response at 50 Hz is ~0.011 arcsec on 0.5 kgm², before flexible amplification. This is not a disturbance specification for the CW0500, and speed-dependent harmonics and structural modes can dominate.')
p('Array and mechanism motion, bearing noise, propellant motion, thruster pulses and thermoelastic alignment need a separate transfer model. A 1 mN thruster misaligned by 1° produces ~2.62 µNm at 15 cm, so propulsion should stay outside the calibrated flash windows unless its response is demonstrated.')
p('Attitude-sensor noise and stray light, gyro drift, the alignment between camera and star tracker, clock correlation and orbit knowledge belong in the measurement covariance. The flight stability of ASTERIA, 0.5 arcsec RMS, used wheel control plus piezo control of the focal plane over 20 minutes; it does not represent this bus or a lunar flash measurement of 1-5 ms. [DE19]')
sub('Science-window consequences')
p('Image motion creates event traffic through d(log I)/dt = ∇log I · v_image. Detector timestamps alone do not establish calibrated temporal photometry. Motion and scene contrast should be tested together with flash injections, and the settling and reacquisition time after unloads and slews must be kept. Gaps from the orbit, thermal and power, navigation, downlink and illumination all add to the availability losses from dumps alone.')
page();head('8. Decisions and verification limits')
table(['Concept action','Evidence / implication','Drivers'],[[x[0]+': '+x[1],x[2],x[3]]for x in [D[0],D[1],D[3],D[4],D[5],D[6]]],[225,190,85])
sub('Actual numerical checks')
table(['Check','Result','Limit'],[['Harmonic J2 sign/normalization','Relative error 2.3e-16','Independent analytical limiting case'],['Finite-difference gradient of the potential','Relative error 1.3e-10','One nonsingular position'],['Two-body energy range over 30 days','Relative 1.7e-12','Not required for the time-dependent multi-body model'],['100 km, gravity 80→120','Day-30 hp changes by 22 m','Concept comparison; not a lifetime certificate'],['100 km, tighter integration','Day-30 hp changes by 2.5 m','60 s maximum step and tenfold tighter rtol; within rerun reproducibility (~1-3 m)'],['100 km, 0.01° frame offset','Day-30 hp changes by 3.2 m','Does not establish exact DE421/430 equivalence; within rerun reproducibility'],['50 km, gravity 80→120','Day-30 hp changes by 0.283 km','The day-48 boundary time is not converged'],['CRTBP invariant / closure','Jacobi range 7.1e-15; period position closure ~1e-5 m','Ideal autonomous model only']],[190,145,165])
p('The minimum next work is to select one science observable and to couple the usable whole-surface exposure and the sample yield to candidate orbits. We then need one dated, maintained trajectory with navigation and execution errors, CAD-based inertia and plate geometry, and a sizing of the wheel and thruster array. Wheel spectra should be propagated through a simple structural and controller model and into the localization covariance. The essential conclusions should take 2-3 pages of the final 50-page mission report.','SmallX')
page();head('9. Source traceability and reproduction')
p('The evidence IDs match the workbook and the Markdown. The dates of the primary sources, their page and table locations and direct links are kept in the evidence register. Flight records, published Monte Carlo and design results, the propagated results of this study and engineering assumptions carry separate labels.')
